# From Protected Information to Discrete Geometry

Begin with the central question: when can the distance of a quantum code be read as a shortest path? Explore the exact arithmetic in the live capacity laboratory.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The geometric dictionary is explicit: a selected source-target distance must equal the code distance. The breadth-first procedure below tests this condition and returns a shortest witness.

{{algorithm:0}}

{{demo:0}}

## The smallest perfect example

The $[[5,1,3]]$ code saturates $2d+k\le n+2$. Its distance is realized by the endpoints of a four-vertex path.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the endpoint-distance proof</summary>
There is a three-edge walk $0	o1	o2	o3$, giving an upper bound of three. Every step in a path changes the vertex label by at most one, so any walk from $0$ to $3$ has at least three edges. Hence the distance is exactly three.
</details>

## Equality is saturation, not a regional entropy theorem

Use the diagnostic to separate exact realization, Singleton validity, and zero slack.

{{algorithm:1}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the precise equivalence</summary>
Under $\operatorname{dist}_G(s,t)=d$, the equations $2\operatorname{dist}_G(s,t)+k=n+2$ and $2d+k=n+2$ have identical left-hand sides. This is a global capacity identity; regional entropy requires additional access-structure data.
</details>

## Why the Tanner graph is not the radial chain

The standard five-qubit presentation has five variable vertices and four check vertices. The radial chain has four vertices.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{visualization:1}}

{{algorithm:2}}

{{demo:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the graph-theoretic obstruction</summary>
Every graph isomorphism induces a bijection of vertex sets. No bijection exists from nine vertices to four, independently of edge incidence.
</details>

## Scaling toward emergent geometry

The defect law $k\le\delta+2$ shows that bounded defect permits only bounded logical capacity. Positive rate therefore requires extensive defect.

{{visualization:2}}

{{demo:3}}

<details><summary>Background and next questions</summary>
Useful introductions include [quantum error correction](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quantum_error_correction), [graph distance](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Distance_(graph_theory)), the [Singleton bound](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Singleton_bound), and the [holographic principle](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Holographic_principle). The open problem is to add locality, recovery data, entropy profiles, and orientation strongly enough to reconstruct curvature and causality.
</details>

